= Memory — Technical =

== Systems and processes ==
Memory taxonomies distinguish systems by content, timescale, conscious accessibility, neural dependence, and task properties.

A useful separation is:

<pre>
encoding → consolidation / retention → retrieval / expression
</pre>

but feedback and reconsolidation make the actual system recurrent rather than linear.

== Episodic and semantic memory ==
Episodic memory supports memory for experienced events in contextual detail.

Semantic memory supports relatively context-independent factual/conceptual knowledge.

The systems interact during learning and retrieval.

== Declarative and nondeclarative memory ==
Declarative memory supports flexible reportable knowledge.

Nondeclarative systems include skills, habits, priming, conditioning, and some perceptual learning.

These categories map imperfectly onto conscious/unconscious distinctions.

== Measurement ==
Recall, recognition, source memory, confidence, response time, savings, priming, neural reinstatement, and behavioral transfer measure different aspects of memory.


== Neurobiological dissociations ==
Research following the case of H.M. helped distinguish medial temporal lobe-dependent declarative learning from multiple other learning systems. Squire and Wixted (2011) review evidence on recollection, familiarity, consolidation and the roles of hippocampal and neighboring structures. Such dissociations do not imply perfectly separate modules. Retrieval can combine remembered content with inference and present context; recognition performance may reflect familiarity, recollection or decision bias.

== Experimental measurement ==
A recognition experiment can present studied and unstudied items, record hits and false alarms, and estimate sensitivity under an explicit signal-detection model. Vary retention interval and interference separately to assess forgetting; delayed failure to retrieve cannot on its own establish loss of a trace. Counterbalance lists, monitor attention and use independent trials. A learning curve measures task performance over exposure and need not identify a particular neural mechanism. Lesion, imaging and behavioral findings provide complementary evidence with distinct confounds.

== Computational comparisons ==
A digital memory store can preserve explicit records and retrieve them by identifier or similarity. Its persistence and provenance can be measured; these properties are not equivalent to biological consolidation, episodic recollection or subjective experience. In a knowledge ecosystem, external records may support memory and coordination while still requiring separate evidence assessment.

